## Structure

```
src/
├── main.ts                  ← starts the server (and ORM connection, if any)
├── server.ts                ← builds the Hapi server; separate from main.ts so tests can use server.inject()
├── config/env.ts            ← the only file that reads process.env
├── plugins/                  ← Hapi plugin registrations (swagger, auth)
├── errors/                   ← AppError base class
├── shared/                   ← database/cache clients used by more than one module
└── routes/<module>/
    ├── <module>.entity.ts      ← the model{{#if ormTypeorm}} (also the TypeORM persistence entity){{/if}}
    ├── <module>.repository.ts  ← data access
    ├── <module>.service.ts     ← business logic
    ├── <module>.handler.ts     ← Hapi route handlers (request, h) => h.response(...)
    ├── <module>.routes.ts      ← wires repository → service → handler → ServerRoute[] (manual, no DI container)
{{#if hasValidation}}
    └── <module>.validation.ts  ← request validation
{{/if}}
```

Call direction: `routes` wires everything → `handler` → `service` → `repository`. A layer only ever talks to the one directly below it.

## Key Hapi Patterns

- Routes export `ServerRoute[]` arrays with `{ method, path, handler }` configs
- Plugins (swagger, auth) are registered via `server.register([...])`
- Handlers return `h.response(data).code(status)` — no `res.send()` or `next()`
- Error handling: services throw `AppError` subclasses, `onPreResponse` ext catches them
- No middleware — use `server.ext()` lifecycle hooks and plugins instead

